.TH PAPERWEIGHT\-SECRETS\-INIT 1 "2026-07-16" "paperweight-secrets 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
paperweight\-secrets\-init \- guided first\-time setup for the PaperweightOS secrets stack
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B paperweight\-secrets\-init
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B paperweight\-secrets\-init
performs the one\-time mechanical setup needed after a GnuPG key exists,
so
.BR pam\-gnupg (8)
can unlock it at login and
.BR pass (1)
has a store to use. Run from a terminal.
.PP
It does
.B not
generate GPG key material itself: if no secret key is found, it prints
instructions to run
.BR gpg (1)'s
.B \-\-full\-generate\-key
and exits, asking to be re\-run afterwards. Key generation needs the
user's own passphrase and entropy, which this script has no business
supplying silently.
.PP
Once a key exists, it:
.IP \(bu 3
extracts the key's keygrips and writes them to
.IR ~/.pam-gnupg ,
one per line;
.IP \(bu 3
ensures
.I ~/.gnupg
is mode 700 and
.I ~/.gnupg/gpg-agent.conf
is mode 600, merging in
.B allow\-preset\-passphrase
and cache\-ttl directives if not already present (existing customizations
are left alone);
.IP \(bu 3
runs
.B pass init
with the key if
.I ~/.password-store
does not already exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.gnupg/gpg-agent.conf
Merged, not overwritten.
.TP
.I ~/.pam-gnupg
Keygrip list consumed by
.BR pam_gnupg.so .
.TP
.I ~/.password-store
Created via
.B pass init
if absent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpg (1),
.BR pass (1),
.BR paperweight\-passmenu (1),
.BR paperweight\-secrets\-sync (1)
